---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';

const description =
	'Senior engineer working in platform engineering, distributed systems, and AI-native development.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About | ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={description} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main class="prose">
			<h1>About</h1>

			<p>
				I'm a senior engineer with 11+ years shipping production systems, most of it in
				platform engineering: designing and running the common infrastructure other teams
				build on, rather than a single product. That's included several years inside a
				Tier-1 European bank, as architect and senior IC across three greenfield platforms
				serving 100+ product teams — GitOps self-service, multi-tenant AWS foundations,
				secure data infrastructure, and event-driven serverless workloads — and, more
				recently, a multi-cloud deployment platform for public-sector tenants in a highly
				regulated, sovereignty-sensitive environment, where I work on GitOps self-service via
				Crossplane, EKS infrastructure handling up to 20,000 req/s, and disaster-recovery
				design.
			</p>

			<p>
				I hold an MSc in Big Data Analytics (multi-agent reinforcement learning), and these
				days that background feeds directly into how I work day to day: AI-native
				development with Claude Code and Kiro, spec-driven and agentic, as a daily driver for
				code, docs, research, and planning — not a novelty layered on top of how I used to
				work, but how the work gets done now.
			</p>

			<p>
				<a href="https://github.com/m-stepkowski/kubemend">kubemend</a> is where those two
				threads meet: an LLM agent harness for Kubernetes remediation, hand-written with no
				agent frameworks, that diagnoses incidents from live cluster state and can only act
				by opening draft pull requests. A verification pipeline the model doesn't control
				re-checks every proposed change — helm render, policy check, live diff, scope check —
				before a run is ever allowed to call itself done. This blog is where I write up what
				building that verification discipline actually costs and what it's caught, with real
				numbers, code, and trace excerpts rather than a demo reel. The posts are meant to
				hold up to someone reading the source they link to, not just the prose.
			</p>

			<p>
				Outside of platform work I build end-to-end on my own: <a
					href="https://getrnkd.app">Rnkd</a
				> is a consumer product I shipped solo — design, mobile frontend, backend,
				infrastructure, distribution — and <a
					href="https://github.com/m-stepkowski/rgw-model">rgw-model</a
				> is a multi-agent reinforcement-learning environment with cooperative and
				competitive agents, closer to my MSc background.
			</p>

			<p>
				I'm a <a href="https://www.cncf.io/training/kubestronaut/">Kubestronaut</a> (all
				five CNCF Kubernetes certifications) and an AWS Certified Solutions Architect –
				Professional. Reach me on <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/maciej-stepkowski"
					>LinkedIn</a
				>.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
<style>
	.prose {
		width: 720px;
		max-width: calc(100% - 2em);
		margin: auto;
		padding: 1em 1em 3em 1em;
	}
</style>
